  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nWe'll continue our quiet forecast to start this week, then shift\nto a more active pattern by the end of the week. The best \nchance of rain comes Friday into Saturday, with the best shot at\nlight snowfall further north across northern Minnesota most \nlikely.\n\nToday through Wednesday...Upper level ridging continues to\ndominate the central US, promoting sunny skies and warming \ntemps. Southwesterly winds ramp up overnight into tomorrow, \nincreasing low-level moisture and potentially throwing a wrench\nin tomorrow's high temperature forecast. Hi-res models are \nreally picking up on this increase in moisture and suggest that \na deck of stratus could push northeast from the central Plains \nand limit how much sun can filter to the surface. We've began \nshifting to this idea, and are leaning towards highs being a few\ndegrees cooler for tomorrow. This means rather than afternoon \ntemps reaching the mid-upper 50s, now expect some areas to \nstruggle reaching that 50 degree mark. The HRRR and the RAP show\nthe most aggressive approach to widespread cloud cover, and as \na result, stunts highs a whole 10 degrees cooler than the NBM. \nIf it starts to look increasingly likely that these low clouds \nwill stick around all day, than those are the models that will \nverify better in more locations.\n\nWinds will become strong again for Wednesday, gusting between 25\nto 35 mph during the afternoon. Cloud cover will play a role \nin temperatures again, but current trends favor clouds clearing \naround midday, which would provide enough warming to climb into \nthe 50s for most.\n\nThursday into Next Weekend...The pattern turns active as the\nridge pushes east and a series of shortwaves ride northeast \nout the Rockies. That first wave comes almost Wednesday night \nnow, but fully moves through the Upper MS Valley Thursday \nmorning. Any concern of rain is almost fully gone now, with the \nNBM PoPs less than 10% across the southern half of Minnesota and\nwestern Wisconsin. The best forcing is further north, close to \nthe international border in NoDak and NW Minnesota. In other \nwords, there is increasing model agreement for the main low \npressure center, and best forcing for any precipitation, to \nremain north of us.\n\nThursday into Friday is more of the in between time before the\nnext wave moves in, and this will be a period where winds die \ndown and clouds clear out more. That period will only last a \nshort 12-18 hours, as winds ramp up again out of the south ahead\nof an approaching Colorado Low. The general consensus across \nensemble and deterministic guidance is for the low to track from\nthe Dakotas into northern Minnesota, similar to Thursday's \nsystem. There will be more moisture to tap into across the warm \nsector this time, however, which is why we are seeing greater \nrain chances and measurable QPF. That said, the best forcing \nremains to the north again, and the footprint of 0.5\" QPF is \nfavored to be closer to northern Minnesota and southern \nOntario. Winter ptypes will almost certainly be apart of this \nsystem, though our best chance of seeing any flakes this weekend\nwill come on Sunday as temperatures crash and any lingering \nmoisture is squeezed out in the CAA regime.",
